1
00:00:00,342 --> 00:00:02,979
Last video ended on this pyramid of tins.

2
00:00:03,462 --> 00:00:06,007
There's one tin on top, then two, then

3
00:00:06,089 --> 00:00:08,494
three, down to fifteen on the bottom row.

4
00:00:09,137 --> 00:00:10,164
How many tins is that?

5
00:00:11,029 --> 00:00:12,813
Adding fifteen numbers one at a time is

6
00:00:13,075 --> 00:00:14,598
slow, and it's easy to slip.

7
00:00:15,261 --> 00:00:17,028
There's a famous trick that does it in

8
00:00:17,210 --> 00:00:17,671
one line.

9
00:00:18,235 --> 00:00:20,501
But a trick isn't much use unless you

10
00:00:20,542 --> 00:00:21,644
know why it works.

11
00:00:22,368 --> 00:00:24,673
So why does it work, and does it

12
00:00:24,714 --> 00:00:26,539
work for every arithmetic sequence?

13
00:00:27,323 --> 00:00:29,339
Pair the top row with the bottom row.

14
00:00:29,843 --> 00:00:31,536
One plus fifteen is sixteen.

15
00:00:32,242 --> 00:00:34,146
Now pair the second row with the second

16
00:00:34,187 --> 00:00:35,910
last: two plus fourteen,

17
00:00:36,432 --> 00:00:37,173
also sixteen.

18
00:00:38,015 --> 00:00:40,118
Every pair gives the same total, because each

19
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:42,323
time you move in one row, the top

20
00:00:42,364 --> 00:00:44,106
number goes up by one and the bottom

21
00:00:44,127 --> 00:00:45,369
number goes down by one.

22
00:00:46,276 --> 00:00:46,799
Here's the picture.

23
00:00:47,423 --> 00:00:49,774
Copy the pyramid, turn it upside down, and

24
00:00:49,896 --> 00:00:51,000
fit it against the first.

25
00:00:51,704 --> 00:00:54,689
Every row now holds sixteen tins, and there

26
00:00:54,710 --> 00:00:57,574
are fifteen rows, so the shape holds fifteen

27
00:00:57,796 --> 00:00:58,777
times sixteen.

28
00:00:59,141 --> 00:01:00,497
That's two hundred and forty.

29
00:01:01,181 --> 00:01:03,512
The pyramid is exactly half of that: one

30
00:01:03,553 --> 00:01:04,517
hundred and twenty tins.

31
00:01:05,302 --> 00:01:08,339
The same thing happens in any arithmetic sequence.

32
00:01:09,043 --> 00:01:11,311
Write the sum forwards, from the first term,

33
00:01:11,613 --> 00:01:13,842
a, up to the last term, a sub

34
00:01:13,903 --> 00:01:13,942
n.

35
00:01:14,647 --> 00:01:17,003
Then write the same sum backwards underneath.

36
00:01:17,806 --> 00:01:20,147
In each column, the top term goes up

37
00:01:20,268 --> 00:01:22,629
by d and the bottom term goes down

38
00:01:22,769 --> 00:01:24,970
by d, so every column adds to a

39
00:01:25,410 --> 00:01:26,271
plus a sub n.

40
00:01:27,112 --> 00:01:29,236
There are n columns, so two lots of

41
00:01:29,257 --> 00:01:32,142
S sub n is n times a plus

42
00:01:32,304 --> 00:01:32,744
a sub n.

43
00:01:33,588 --> 00:01:35,675
Halve it, and S sub n is n

44
00:01:35,756 --> 00:01:38,165
over two, times a plus a sub n.

45
00:01:39,008 --> 00:01:41,252
In words, it's the average of the first

46
00:01:41,353 --> 00:01:43,718
and last terms, times how many terms there

47
00:01:43,779 --> 00:01:43,878
are.

48
00:01:44,408 --> 00:01:46,116
Often you don't know the last term.

49
00:01:46,619 --> 00:01:48,824
But you do know it's a, plus n

50
00:01:48,885 --> 00:01:50,348
minus one lots of d.

51
00:01:51,171 --> 00:01:52,997
Swap that in for a sub n, and

52
00:01:53,118 --> 00:01:55,827
the bracket becomes two a, plus n minus

53
00:01:56,009 --> 00:01:56,911
one lots of d.

54
00:01:57,735 --> 00:02:00,001
So S sub n is also n over

55
00:02:00,042 --> 00:02:03,171
two, times two a plus n minus one

56
00:02:03,392 --> 00:02:03,953
lots of d.

57
00:02:04,757 --> 00:02:07,230
It's the same sum, written with a, d

58
00:02:07,532 --> 00:02:07,913
and n.

59
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:09,686
Test it on the pyramid.

60
00:02:10,230 --> 00:02:12,776
With a and d both one, and n

61
00:02:12,898 --> 00:02:15,906
equal to fifteen, it's fifteen over two, times

62
00:02:16,127 --> 00:02:17,170
two plus fourteen.

63
00:02:17,853 --> 00:02:20,304
That's seven and a half times sixteen, which

64
00:02:20,365 --> 00:02:21,651
is one hundred and twenty again.

65
00:02:22,575 --> 00:02:23,917
Use the first form when you know the

66
00:02:23,958 --> 00:02:26,103
last term, and the second when you know

67
00:02:26,164 --> 00:02:27,065
the common difference.

68
00:02:27,748 --> 00:02:29,912
Find the sum of the first twenty terms

69
00:02:30,193 --> 00:02:32,578
of three, seven, eleven, and so on.

70
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:35,495
Here a is three, d is four, and

71
00:02:35,718 --> 00:02:36,462
n is twenty.

72
00:02:37,005 --> 00:02:38,670
We don't know the last term, so use

73
00:02:38,711 --> 00:02:39,553
the second form.

74
00:02:39,876 --> 00:02:43,184
That's ten, times six plus nineteen fours.

75
00:02:43,867 --> 00:02:46,720
Nineteen fours is seventy-six, so it's ten times

76
00:02:46,822 --> 00:02:48,750
eighty-two, which is eight hundred and twenty.

77
00:02:49,495 --> 00:02:50,319
Check with the first form.

78
00:02:50,782 --> 00:02:53,623
The twentieth term is three plus seventy-six, which

79
00:02:53,664 --> 00:02:56,725
is seventy-nine, and ten times three plus seventy-nine

80
00:02:56,906 --> 00:02:58,326
is also eight hundred and twenty.

81
00:02:59,101 --> 00:03:01,207
Now, the sum of all the whole numbers

82
00:03:01,288 --> 00:03:02,893
from fifty to two hundred.

83
00:03:03,778 --> 00:03:05,973
It's arithmetic, with d equal to one.

84
00:03:06,616 --> 00:03:08,425
The first term is fifty, and the last

85
00:03:08,566 --> 00:03:10,776
is two hundred, so the first form fits.

86
00:03:11,422 --> 00:03:12,976
But how many terms are there?

87
00:03:13,461 --> 00:03:14,428
Not one hundred and fifty.

88
00:03:14,931 --> 00:03:17,998
It's two hundred minus fifty, plus one, which

89
00:03:18,038 --> 00:03:19,261
is one hundred and fifty-one.

90
00:03:20,065 --> 00:03:21,933
So the sum is one hundred and fifty-one

91
00:03:22,074 --> 00:03:24,464
over two, times fifty plus two hundred.

92
00:03:25,308 --> 00:03:27,632
That's one hundred and fifty-one, times one hundred

93
00:03:27,653 --> 00:03:30,538
and twenty-five, which is eighteen thousand, eight hundred

94
00:03:30,559 --> 00:03:31,239
and seventy-five.

95
00:03:31,890 --> 00:03:32,332
Sometimes n

96
00:03:33,059 --> 00:03:33,784
is the unknown.

97
00:03:34,489 --> 00:03:37,132
How many terms of four, plus nine, plus

98
00:03:37,214 --> 00:03:39,517
fourteen, and so on, are needed for the

99
00:03:39,578 --> 00:03:41,581
sum to first go over five hundred?

100
00:03:42,403 --> 00:03:44,846
Here a is four and d is five,

101
00:03:45,328 --> 00:03:47,471
so S sub n is n over two,

102
00:03:47,552 --> 00:03:49,174
times five n plus three.

103
00:03:50,016 --> 00:03:51,961
Set that above five hundred, and you get

104
00:03:52,002 --> 00:03:53,304
a quadratic inequality.

105
00:03:54,027 --> 00:03:56,655
Its positive root is about thirteen point eight

106
00:03:56,737 --> 00:03:57,037
five.

107
00:03:57,741 --> 00:03:59,950
But n must be a whole number, so

108
00:03:59,991 --> 00:04:01,719
the first one that works is fourteen.

109
00:04:02,406 --> 00:04:03,155
Check both sides.

110
00:04:03,761 --> 00:04:06,165
Thirteen terms make four hundred and forty-two, which

111
00:04:06,366 --> 00:04:06,886
isn't enough,

112
00:04:07,428 --> 00:04:10,113
and fourteen terms make five hundred and eleven.

113
00:04:10,896 --> 00:04:13,380
Because every term is positive, the sum only

114
00:04:13,441 --> 00:04:15,525
grows, so fourteen is the first.

115
00:04:16,210 --> 00:04:16,934
Here's a surprise.

116
00:04:17,458 --> 00:04:20,389
How many terms of eleven, plus nine, plus

117
00:04:20,410 --> 00:04:22,918
seven, and so on, add up to thirty-two?

118
00:04:23,742 --> 00:04:26,525
Here a is eleven and d is minus

119
00:04:26,586 --> 00:04:29,410
two, so S sub n simplifies to n

120
00:04:29,551 --> 00:04:31,213
times twelve minus n.

121
00:04:32,015 --> 00:04:34,340
Set that equal to thirty-two, and the quadratic

122
00:04:34,421 --> 00:04:35,122
has two roots:

123
00:04:35,664 --> 00:04:36,686
four and eight.

124
00:04:37,550 --> 00:04:39,502
Both are whole numbers, and both work.

125
00:04:40,531 --> 00:04:41,664
Four terms make thirty-two.

126
00:04:42,250 --> 00:04:45,338
The next four terms are three, one, minus

127
00:04:45,419 --> 00:04:47,404
one and minus three, and they add to

128
00:04:47,445 --> 00:04:49,891
zero, so eight terms make thirty-two again.

129
00:04:50,694 --> 00:04:52,418
When the terms go negative, the sum can

130
00:04:52,460 --> 00:04:54,645
come back down, so check every root.

131
00:04:55,325 --> 00:04:56,239
Here's the short version.

132
00:04:56,741 --> 00:04:59,024
An arithmetic sum is the average of the

133
00:04:59,104 --> 00:05:01,726
first and last terms, times how many terms

134
00:05:01,767 --> 00:05:02,087
there are.

135
00:05:02,788 --> 00:05:05,610
It's guaranteed for arithmetic sequences, because their terms

136
00:05:05,790 --> 00:05:07,052
always pair up evenly.

137
00:05:07,753 --> 00:05:08,841
Other lists might not.

138
00:05:09,624 --> 00:05:11,790
For one, plus two, plus four,

139
00:05:12,311 --> 00:05:13,514
it gives seven and a half,

140
00:05:14,035 --> 00:05:15,579
but the real sum is seven.

141
00:05:18,250 --> 00:05:18,735
Your turn.

142
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:21,366
Find the sum of the first thirty terms

143
00:05:21,507 --> 00:05:24,617
of the arithmetic series five, plus eight, plus

144
00:05:24,678 --> 00:05:25,841
eleven, and so on.

145
00:05:26,447 --> 00:05:27,259
Pause here to answer.

146
00:05:37,901 --> 00:05:38,202
Here a

147
00:05:38,765 --> 00:05:39,247
is five,

148
00:05:39,769 --> 00:05:40,593
d is three

149
00:05:41,115 --> 00:05:42,321
and n is thirty.

150
00:05:43,084 --> 00:05:46,193
The thirtieth term is five plus twenty-nine threes,

151
00:05:46,534 --> 00:05:47,376
which is ninety-two.

152
00:05:48,138 --> 00:05:49,499
So the sum is fifteen,

153
00:05:50,140 --> 00:05:51,901
times five plus ninety-two,

154
00:05:52,482 --> 00:05:55,204
which is one thousand four hundred and fifty-five.

155
00:05:56,089 --> 00:05:57,244
Fifteen hundred is wrong.

156
00:05:57,768 --> 00:06:00,137
It uses thirty steps of three, instead of

157
00:06:00,217 --> 00:06:00,779
twenty-nine.

158
00:06:02,443 --> 00:06:02,827
Last one.

159
00:06:03,632 --> 00:06:06,617
How many terms of two, plus six, plus

160
00:06:06,657 --> 00:06:07,899
ten, and so on,

161
00:06:08,420 --> 00:06:10,364
are needed for the sum to first be

162
00:06:10,464 --> 00:06:11,706
more than two hundred?

163
00:06:12,489 --> 00:06:13,317
Pause here to answer.

164
00:06:23,868 --> 00:06:24,168
Here a

165
00:06:24,671 --> 00:06:26,356
is two and d is four,

166
00:06:26,859 --> 00:06:29,267
so S sub n simplifies to two n

167
00:06:29,308 --> 00:06:29,649
squared.

168
00:06:30,392 --> 00:06:32,176
Two n squared is more than two hundred

169
00:06:32,277 --> 00:06:34,141
when n squared is more than a hundred,

170
00:06:34,503 --> 00:06:35,846
so n is more than ten.

171
00:06:36,669 --> 00:06:39,537
Ten terms make exactly two hundred, which isn't

172
00:06:39,619 --> 00:06:39,838
more.

173
00:06:40,542 --> 00:06:42,257
So the answer is eleven terms.

174
00:06:42,945 --> 00:06:43,490
Ten is wrong.

175
00:06:44,056 --> 00:06:45,944
It reaches two hundred, but doesn't go over.

176
00:06:47,615 --> 00:06:50,364
So pairing explains both sum formulas.

177
00:06:51,229 --> 00:06:51,551
Next,

178
00:06:52,218 --> 00:06:53,064
real situations.

179
00:06:53,930 --> 00:06:56,371
A volunteer crew plants fifty seedlings on its

180
00:06:56,412 --> 00:06:56,932
first day,

181
00:06:57,493 --> 00:06:59,434
eighty on the second and a hundred and

182
00:06:59,515 --> 00:07:02,036
ten on the third, thirty more each day.

183
00:07:02,939 --> 00:07:05,506
After twelve days, have they planted more or

184
00:07:05,627 --> 00:07:07,412
less than twelve hundred seedlings?

185
00:07:10,221 --> 00:07:11,105
Here are the key notes.

186
00:07:11,627 --> 00:07:13,396
Pause the video if you need more time

187
00:07:13,416 --> 00:07:13,898
to copy them.
